## Presentation Outline

### Slide 1 — Title
Store Inventory System
- Group members
- Course and section
- Date

### Slide 2 — Problem and Goal
- Manual deployment is slow and risky
- Need an automated pipeline that builds, tests, and deploys code safely
- Goal: show Docker + Jenkins + CI/CD in action

### Slide 3 — System Architecture
- Frontend → NGINX → backend services
- PostgreSQL for persistent storage
- Jenkins pipeline for automation

### Slide 4 — Modules
- Frontend UI
- Inventory API
- Orders API
- PostgreSQL database
- Reverse proxy

### Slide 5 — Why These Technologies
- Node.js for backend APIs
- PostgreSQL for reliable persistence
- Docker Compose for orchestration
- Jenkins for automation
- NGINX for routing traffic

### Slide 6 — Dockerfiles & Containers
- Explain each container and Dockerfile role
- Emphasize pinned versions and small base images

### Slide 7 — Docker Compose Orchestration
- Services, environment variables, health checks, volume mounts
- Data persistence proof

### Slide 8 — Jenkins Setup
- Jenkins runs inside Docker
- Docker Pipeline plugin installed
- Docker socket access for CI/CD jobs

### Slide 9 — Pipeline Workflow
- Checkout → Test → Build Images → Deploy → Smoke Test
- Each stage explained

### Slide 10 — Quality Gates
- Failing tests stop deployment
- Broken code never reaches the running app
- Show how previous version stays live

### Slide 11 — Demo Flow
- Start the stack
- Show app in browser
- Push a code change
- Jenkins triggers automatically
- Browser refresh shows updated version

### Slide 12 — Data Persistence and Rollback
- Records remain after restart
- Old image tags can be redeployed

### Slide 13 — Challenges Encountered
- Duplicate key errors in tests
- NGINX proxy path errors
- Docker networking and health checks
- How these were resolved

